---
import DocsLayout from "../../../layouts/DocsLayout.astro";
import { Code } from "astro-expressive-code/components";
---

<DocsLayout title="Auth Model">
  <h1>Auth Model</h1>
  <p>
    Identity issues access tokens (short-lived JWTs) and refresh tokens
    (opaque, server-side). Downstream services trust the JWT; only Identity
    can mint or revoke one.
  </p>

  <h2>Token roles</h2>
  <table>
    <tr><th>Token</th><th>Format</th><th>Lifetime</th><th>Purpose</th></tr>
    <tr>
      <td>Access token</td>
      <td>RS256 JWT, <code>kid</code> header</td>
      <td><code>GATEWAY_JWT_EXPIRY_SECONDS</code> (default <code>900</code> = 15 min)</td>
      <td>Sent on every authenticated RPC as <code>Authorization: Bearer &lt;token&gt;</code>.</td>
    </tr>
    <tr>
      <td>Refresh token</td>
      <td>Opaque random string, hashed in DB</td>
      <td><code>GATEWAY_REFRESH_EXPIRY_SECONDS</code> (default <code>604800</code> = 7 days)</td>
      <td>Sent only to <code>RefreshToken</code> / <code>Logout</code>. Rotated on every use.</td>
    </tr>
  </table>

  <h2>Access token claims</h2>
  <Code code={`{
  "iss": "identity",
  "sub": "user_01HZ...",       // user ID
  "tenant": "my-product",       // GATEWAY_DEFAULT_TENANT_ID
  "role": "admin",              // "admin" | "member" | "guest"
  "iat": 1730000000,
  "exp": 1730000900,
  "jti": "..."
}`} lang="json" />
  <p>
    Access tokens intentionally carry identity and a coarse
    <code>role</code> only. Identity does not embed per-group membership
    or app-specific ACL decisions into the JWT.
  </p>
  <p>
    <code>VerifyAccessToken</code> in <code>pkg/jwt</code> checks the
    signature, <code>exp</code>, <code>iss</code>, and looks the
    <code>kid</code> up in the active key ring. Tokens signed with a key
    that has been rotated out (but is still in the ring) continue to verify
    until they expire — see <a href="/identity/docs/installation/jwt-keys">JWT Keys</a>.
  </p>

  <h2>Bearer-token middleware</h2>
  <p>
    <code>internal/middleware/auth.go</code> verifies the bearer token on
    every request whose path is not in <code>AuthExemptPaths</code>. On
    success it sets <code>X-Authenticated-User-Id</code> on the request so
    the Connect handler can read the caller without re-parsing the JWT.
  </p>

  <h3>Exempt paths</h3>
  <ul>
    <li><code>BeginOAuthLogin</code>, <code>OAuthLogin</code>, <code>PasswordLogin</code>, <code>PasswordSignup</code>, <code>RefreshToken</code>, <code>Logout</code>, <code>GetCurrentUser</code></li>
    <li><code>RequestEmailLoginCode</code>, <code>VerifyEmailLoginCode</code>, <code>RequestMagicLink</code>, <code>RedeemMagicLink</code> (passwordless)</li>
    <li><code>BeginPasskeyLogin</code>, <code>CompletePasskeyLogin</code></li>
    <li><code>InitiateQrLogin</code>, <code>PollQrLogin</code></li>
    <li><code>AcceptInvitation</code>, <code>RequestAdminHelp</code>, <code>VerifyTotp</code></li>
    <li><code>/.well-known/jwks.json</code>, <code>/health</code>, <code>/healthz</code></li>
  </ul>
  <p>
    Even on exempt paths, the middleware still <em>parses</em> a bearer
    token if one is supplied (so <code>GetCurrentUser</code> can return the
    authenticated identity when called) — it just never rejects the request
    for missing/invalid auth.
  </p>

  <h2>Roles</h2>
  <p>
    The <code>role</code> claim is opaque to Identity except in two places:
  </p>
  <ul>
    <li>
      <strong>Admin RPCs</strong> require <code>role=admin</code>. The Connect
      handler enforces this per-method and audit-logs the actor.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>The first user</strong> created in a fresh deployment is
      auto-promoted to <code>admin</code> so a freshly-deployed instance is
      not lockable-out. Subsequent users default to <code>member</code>.
    </li>
  </ul>
  <p>
    Downstream services may interpret <code>role</code> however they want.
    Identity does not authorize per-resource access; it authenticates and
    declares a coarse role.
  </p>

  <h2>Authorization boundary</h2>
  <p>
    Identity's authorization boundary is intentionally narrow:
    authentication, JWT issuance, coarse roles, and group membership data.
  </p>
  <ul>
    <li>
      Identity itself enforces only coarse built-in checks such as
      <code>role=admin</code> on admin and group-management RPCs.
    </li>
    <li>
      Groups and memberships are identity data, not permissions. A group
      membership does not grant access to any RPC or resource by itself.
    </li>
    <li>
      If a product needs rules like "members of group X may access
      resource Y", that ACL belongs in the calling app or a separate
      permissions layer above Identity.
    </li>
  </ul>

  <h2>Lockout</h2>
  <p>
    Failed-login lockout is configured by
    <code>GATEWAY_LOGIN_MAX_FAILED_ATTEMPTS</code> (default <code>5</code>) and
    <code>GATEWAY_LOGIN_LOCKOUT_SECONDS</code> (default <code>900</code> = 15 min).
    On the Nth failure within the window, <code>PasswordLogin</code>
    returns <code>account temporarily locked</code> until the timer
    expires; correct credentials reset the counter.
  </p>

  <h2>Email Verification Policy</h2>
  <p>
    OAuth and local password accounts do not use the same verification gate.
  </p>
  <ul>
    <li>
      <strong>OAuth login</strong> requires the upstream provider to assert a
      verified email address before Identity will mint a session.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>Password login</strong> does not block on
      <code>email_verified</code>. A local user may sign in immediately after
      signup and complete verification later in-app.
    </li>
  </ul>

  <h2>Signup Enumeration Safety</h2>
  <p>
    <code>PasswordSignup</code> is intentionally not an account-existence
    oracle. When the email is new, Identity creates the user, sends the
    verification email, and returns the usual signup response. When the
    email already exists, Identity still returns a success-shaped payload,
    sends a notice email to the existing address, and does not create a
    second user or mint a durable session for the caller.
  </p>

  <h2>Replica consistency</h2>
  <p>
    Security-sensitive auth state is repo-backed rather than replica-local.
    That includes failed-login counters, refresh-token rotation and replay
    detection, passkey challenges, TOTP enrollment and login challenges,
    QR login sessions, and the various email-token flows.
  </p>
  <p>
    The remaining process-local auth inputs are boot-time configuration and
    cryptographic material: JWT keys, the TOTP encryption key, passkey RP
    config, OAuth provider credentials, and the self-serve password toggles.
    Multi-replica deployments therefore require consistent rollout of those
    values across every replica.
  </p>

  <h2>Self-Serve Password Toggles</h2>
  <p>
    <code>GATEWAY_PASSWORD_SIGNUP_ENABLED</code> and
    <code>GATEWAY_PASSWORD_RESET_ENABLED</code> are deployment-scoped flags.
    Identity reads them once at process start. They are not hot-reloaded,
    and there is no repo-backed runtime feature-flag model for them today.
  </p>
  <p>
    Changing either toggle in a multi-replica deployment requires a rolling
    restart. During the rollout window, old replicas continue serving the
    previous behavior until they drain. The operator runbook lives at
    <a href="/identity/docs/operations/password-toggle-rollout">Password Toggle Rollout</a>.
  </p>
</DocsLayout>
